30 Days of Ansible – Day 5/30

Ansible Ad-hoc Commands – Automate Tasks Without Writing a Playbook

Did you know you can execute tasks on hundreds of servers without creating a playbook?

That's exactly what Ansible Ad-hoc Commands are designed for.

They are one-line commands that help you perform quick administrative tasks such as checking connectivity, installing packages, restarting services, copying files, and gathering system information.

When should you use Ad-hoc Commands?

Ad-hoc commands are perfect for:

- Verifying server connectivity
- Restarting services
- Installing packages
- Copying files
- Creating users
- Collecting system information

For repetitive or complex tasks, use Ansible Playbooks instead.

Common Ad-hoc Commands

1️⃣ Check Connectivity

ansible all -m ping

2️⃣ Check Disk Space

ansible all -a "df -h"

3️⃣ Check Memory Usage

ansible all -a "free -m"

4️⃣ Restart Nginx Service

ansible webservers -m service -a "name=nginx state=restarted" --become

5️⃣ Install Git

ansible all -m package -a "name=git state=present" --become

Real-World Example

Imagine your production team discovers that the Nginx service has stopped on 50 web servers.

Without Ansible:

- SSH into each server.
- Restart the service manually.
- Verify the status.

With Ansible:

ansible webservers -m service -a "name=nginx state=restarted" --become

One command. Fifty servers. A few seconds.

This is why DevOps engineers rely on Ansible for day-to-day operations.

Key Takeaway

Ad-hoc commands are ideal for quick, one-time tasks across multiple servers. They save time, reduce manual effort, and make infrastructure management much more efficient.
